WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.854
FFI is a common place where Rust's
ownership model meets external cleanup rules.

2
00:00:03.934 --> 00:00:05.512
A C library, platform API, device SDK,

3
00:00:05.592 --> 00:00:08.749
or native runtime may allocate a resource
that must be released by a matching

4
00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:09.618
cleanup function.

5
00:00:09.698 --> 00:00:12.756
Rust cannot automatically infer every rule
from the external interface.

6
00:00:12.836 --> 00:00:14.463
Allocator compatibility is critical.

7
00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:17.207
Some objects must be freed by the same
library that created them.

8
00:00:17.287 --> 00:00:20.098
Some buffers must be returned through a
specific release function.

9
00:00:20.178 --> 00:00:21.559
Some handles must be closed once.

10
00:00:21.639 --> 00:00:23.415
If Rust allocates and external code frees,

11
00:00:23.495 --> 00:00:25.270
or external code allocates and Rust frees,

12
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.274
the contract must be explicit and
compatible.

13
00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:30.066
A wrapper should also make ownership
transition points obvious.

14
00:00:30.146 --> 00:00:31.873
If an external function takes ownership,

15
00:00:31.953 --> 00:00:35.208
the Rust wrapper should consume the owner
or move it into a state where ordinary

16
00:00:35.288 --> 00:00:36.521
callers cannot drop it again.

17
00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:38.426
If external code only borrows temporarily,

18
00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:41.218
the wrapper should not surrender cleanup
authority by accident.

19
00:00:41.298 --> 00:00:43.419
Error paths can change ownership
responsibility.

20
00:00:43.499 --> 00:00:46.163
An external function might transfer
ownership only on success,

21
00:00:46.243 --> 00:00:48.610
or it might require cleanup even after a
partial failure.

22
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.896
A wrapper should document these cases and
encode them so callers do not have to

23
00:00:51.976 --> 00:00:54.195
remember subtle release rules under error
pressure.

24
00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:57.284
Callbacks and retained pointers create
additional cleanup questions.

25
00:00:57.364 --> 00:01:00.521
External code may store a pointer for
later use, borrow only during the call,

26
00:01:00.601 --> 00:01:01.834
or take ownership of a handle.

27
00:01:01.914 --> 00:01:05.415
The wrapper should make those modes
distinct and prevent duplicate cleanup or stale

28
00:01:05.495 --> 00:01:06.433
retained references.

29
00:01:06.513 --> 00:01:09.176
A safe FFI wrapper should prevent both
leaks and double cleanup.

30
00:01:09.256 --> 00:01:12.560
It should know who allocates, who frees,
which function releases the resource,

31
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.353
when ownership transfers, and how cleanup
behaves after errors.

32
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:18.935
That knowledge belongs in types,
constructors, `Drop`, and safety documentation,

33
00:01:19.015 --> 00:01:20.100
not only in tribal memory.
